C LAB 4 - CHAR ARRAYS, INDEXING, ADDRESSING

GOAL: Write bytes from local storage instead of a literal buffer.
Write /tmp/buf.c via write_file:

  int main() {
      char s[4] = "ABC";
      s[1] = 'x';
      write(1, s, 3);
      write(1, "\n", 1);
      return 0;
  }

Run: cc /tmp/buf.c; as /tmp/buf.s; /tmp/a.out.
Expected stdout: AxC and newline.

This is a single-dimensional char array. The string initializer
requires sufficient storage for the terminator. No dynamic
allocation, string library, memcpy or printf are available here.
Avoid writing past the declared array; no runtime bounds check.
Inspect generated assembly with cat /tmp/buf.s if necessary.
